{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WBPmfSyGBsG92vVCLi76cU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\begin{abstract}\n2\tCompound sparsity---simultaneously pruning attention heads and evicting KV cache entries---is essential for efficient LLM inference but produces super-additive quality degradation whose structure is poorly understood.\n3\tWe find that compound sparsity severity is not a fixed constant but an emergent property of the interaction among eviction strategy, reasoning phase, and sparsity level.\n4\tOn DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (MATH-500), importance-based eviction exhibits a monotonically increasing phase--severity gradient: the paired median difference $\\Delta$ rises from $0.39$ at sparsity $s{=}0.15$ to $8.48$ at $s{=}0.30$ (Spearman $\\rho{=}1.0$; all Bonferroni-corrected $p < 0.005$).\n5\tRandom eviction develops a detectable gradient only above $s \\approx 0.20$, at $2.7$--$17{\\times}$ smaller magnitude.\n6\tThe pattern replicates on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct ($\\Delta$: $0.07 \\to 3.16$; $\\rho{=}1.0$), shows consistent phase gradient direction on GSM8K for both models, and generalizes to LlamaForCausalLM ($n{=}250$; monotonic gradient confirmed, ${\\sim}25{\\times}$ smaller magnitude).\n7\tPer-phase Gini analysis identifies \\emph{importance discrimination collapse}---the degeneration of head importance scores toward uniformity under compound stress---as a correlational diagnostic of this severity divergence.\n8\tPrior work characterizes compound degradation as phase-agnostic; we show that phase modulates severity by $15$--$17{\\times}$ on DeepSeek and $2.7$--$3.5{\\times}$ on Qwen. These findings establish phase-gated analysis---decomposing compound severity by reasoning phase and eviction strategy---as a principled diagnostic framework, with direct implications for phase-aware budget allocation in inference systems.\n9\t\\end{abstract}\n10\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lwe9c8VKc2fkXMotvhqgLJ","session_id":"93355213-87f8-4b5a-b04b-1b0ebb649ece","uuid":"d9b3fb37-8615-445a-93dd-51c48fdeeab5","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:33:56.891Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HTbzodUpWskmpEgn2Vjm1h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012pREfH3yt3uFaMYKGsht9g","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. \n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\n- Overall assessment scale: 1–5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues)\n- Three dimensions: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Recommendation\n- Acceptance rate: ~22%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Phase-Stratified Diagnosis of Compound Sparsity Interactions: The Interaction Index Framework\"\n\nThe paper introduces the Interaction Index (II), a per-problem paired diagnostic metric with bootstrap CIs and phase-stratified decomposition, for diagnosing compound sparsity (attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) interactions in LLMs. Key claims:\n\n1. **II metric** (Eq 1): II_i(s,e) = Δ_compound,i − (Δ_prune,i + Δ_evict,i). This is the per-problem interaction term from a 2×2 factorial design.\n2. **Phase stratification** (Eq 2): G(s,e) = median_i(II_low,i) − median_i(II_high,i), where low=0-33% tokens, high=67-100% tokens.\n3. **Phase gradient universality**: within-importance dz > 0.95 across 3 architectures.\n4. **Strategy specificity is context-dependent**: 15–36× on MATH-500, 2.3–3.8× on GSM8K, 1.1–1.3× on GPQA; absent on LLaMA.\n5. **Discrimination collapse**: Gini–II within-model ρ=1.0 (4 points), cross-model r=0.48 (NS).\n\n## Key Related Work the Paper Must Differentiate From\n- **Physics of KV Cache Compression** (2603.01426): Theoretical framework for compression-rate phase transitions and representational rigidity. Their \"phase\" = critical compression thresholds; this paper's \"phase\" = temporal reasoning stages. But the practical insight is similar: \"compression hurts more at certain stages.\"\n- **HIES**: Identifies importance score collapse in single-axis settings with entropy compensation. This paper's \"discrimination collapse\" extends to compound setting — but is this a straightforward extension?\n- **ThinKV**: CoT-phase-aware KV cache eviction on single axis. Already does phase-aware compression.\n- **SparK / SLiM**: Joint head pruning + KV eviction but treat interaction as uniform.\n- **Ada-KV**: Adaptive cache budget allocation without phase stratification.\n- **R-KV**: KV compression optimized for reasoning tasks.\n\n## Experimental Coverage Assessment\nFrom the review context YAML, the paper covers:\n- 3 architectures (DeepSeek-Qwen, Qwen, DeepSeek-LLaMA) — all 7B scale\n- 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond)\n- Strategy specificity collapses on GPQA (ratio 1.1-1.3×) and on LLaMA (dz<0.20)\n- The narrative had to be reframed from \"strategy×phase interaction as core finding\" to \"phase gradient as universal, strategy specificity as one context-dependent manifestation\"\n- Cross-model Gini-II correlation is NS (r=0.48)\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points)\nFocus on positioning, clarity of contribution, related work coverage.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points)\nFocus on:\n- **Novelty concern 1**: Physics of KV Cache Compression already characterizes phase-dependent compression behavior. This paper's phase gradient in compound setting could be seen as a special case.\n- **Novelty concern 2**: HIES already identified importance score collapse. \"Discrimination collapse\" in compound setting is a predictable extension.\n- **Novelty concern 3**: The II metric is just the standard interaction term from factorial experimental design — calling it \"Interaction Index\" doesn't add methodological novelty.\n- **Scope concern**: Strategy specificity (initially a core claim) doesn't hold on LLaMA or GPQA, reducing the three-dimensional (compound × phase × strategy) framing to two dimensions (compound × phase), which is closer to what Physics of KV Cache already covers.\n- **Scale**: Only 7B models. No evidence this generalizes to 13B, 70B, or MoE architectures.\n- **Related work positioning**: The \"orthogonal dimension\" argument is clever but may not convince reviewers — the practical insight (\"compression hurts more in early reasoning phases\") overlaps substantially with phase-aware compression literature.\n- **Broken references**: Multiple \"??\" and \"?\" in citations throughout the paper.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n### 5. Scores\n- Soundness (1-5)\n- Excitement (1-5)\n- Overall Assessment (1-5)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the contribution clears the novelty bar for a top-tier venue with ~22% acceptance rate. Consider whether this is better suited for Findings track or a workshop."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2300,"cache_read_input_tokens":54322,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2300},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93355213-87f8-4b5a-b04b-1b0ebb649ece","uuid":"f829ef1c-e166-4254-bf45-fbc13d0ef02d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a8fe8cd58084698b0","tool_use_id":"toolu_012pREfH3yt3uFaMYKGsht9g","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. \n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\n- Overall assessment scale: 1–5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues)\n- Three dimensions: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Recommendation\n- Acceptance rate: ~22%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Phase-Stratified Diagnosis of Compound Sparsity Interactions: The Interaction Index Framework\"\n\nThe paper introduces the Interaction Index (II), a per-problem paired diagnostic metric with bootstrap CIs and phase-stratified decomposition, for diagnosing compound sparsity (attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) interactions in LLMs. Key claims:\n\n1. **II metric** (Eq 1): II_i(s,e) = Δ_compound,i − (Δ_prune,i + Δ_evict,i). This is the per-problem interaction term from a 2×2 factorial design.\n2. **Phase stratification** (Eq 2): G(s,e) = median_i(II_low,i) − median_i(II_high,i), where low=0-33% tokens, high=67-100% tokens.\n3. **Phase gradient universality**: within-importance dz > 0.95 across 3 architectures.\n4. **Strategy specificity is context-dependent**: 15–36× on MATH-500, 2.3–3.8× on GSM8K, 1.1–1.3× on GPQA; absent on LLaMA.\n5. **Discrimination collapse**: Gini–II within-model ρ=1.0 (4 points), cross-model r=0.48 (NS).\n\n## Key Related Work the Paper Must Differentiate From\n- **Physics of KV Cache Compression** (2603.01426): Theoretical framework for compression-rate phase transitions and representational rigidity. Their \"phase\" = critical compression thresholds; this paper's \"phase\" = temporal reasoning stages. But the practical insight is similar: \"compression hurts more at certain stages.\"\n- **HIES**: Identifies importance score collapse in single-axis settings with entropy compensation. This paper's \"discrimination collapse\" extends to compound setting — but is this a straightforward extension?\n- **ThinKV**: CoT-phase-aware KV cache eviction on single axis. Already does phase-aware compression.\n- **SparK / SLiM**: Joint head pruning + KV eviction but treat interaction as uniform.\n- **Ada-KV**: Adaptive cache budget allocation without phase stratification.\n- **R-KV**: KV compression optimized for reasoning tasks.\n\n## Experimental Coverage Assessment\nFrom the review context YAML, the paper covers:\n- 3 architectures (DeepSeek-Qwen, Qwen, DeepSeek-LLaMA) — all 7B scale\n- 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond)\n- Strategy specificity collapses on GPQA (ratio 1.1-1.3×) and on LLaMA (dz<0.20)\n- The narrative had to be reframed from \"strategy×phase interaction as core finding\" to \"phase gradient as universal, strategy specificity as one context-dependent manifestation\"\n- Cross-model Gini-II correlation is NS (r=0.48)\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points)\nFocus on positioning, clarity of contribution, related work coverage.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points)\nFocus on:\n- **Novelty concern 1**: Physics of KV Cache Compression already characterizes phase-dependent compression behavior. This paper's phase gradient in compound setting could be seen as a special case.\n- **Novelty concern 2**: HIES already identified importance score collapse. \"Discrimination collapse\" in compound setting is a predictable extension.\n- **Novelty concern 3**: The II metric is just the standard interaction term from factorial experimental design — calling it \"Interaction Index\" doesn't add methodological novelty.\n- **Scope concern**: Strategy specificity (initially a core claim) doesn't hold on LLaMA or GPQA, reducing the three-dimensional (compound × phase × strategy) framing to two dimensions (compound × phase), which is closer to what Physics of KV Cache already covers.\n- **Scale**: Only 7B models. No evidence this generalizes to 13B, 70B, or MoE architectures.\n- **Related work positioning**: The \"orthogonal dimension\" argument is clever but may not convince reviewers — the practical insight (\"compression hurts more in early reasoning phases\") overlaps substantially with phase-aware compression literature.\n- **Broken references**: Multiple \"??\" and \"?\" in citations throughout the paper.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n### 5. Scores\n- Soundness (1-5)\n- Excitement (1-5)\n- Overall Assessment (1-5)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the contribution clears the novelty bar for a top-tier venue with ~22% acceptance rate. Consider whether this is better suited for Findings track or a workshop.","uuid":"9ed0fe26-a73f-4c9d-b525-7710a9a76865","session_id":"93355213-87f8-4b5a-b04b-1b0ebb649ece"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. \n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\n- Overall assessment scale: 1–5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues)\n- Three dimensions: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Recommendation\n- Acceptance rate: ~22%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Phase-Stratified Diagnosis of Compound Sparsity Interactions: The Interaction Index Framework\"\n\nThe paper introduces the Interaction Index (II), a per-problem paired diagnostic metric with bootstrap CIs and phase-stratified decomposition, for diagnosing compound sparsity (attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) interactions in LLMs. Key claims:\n\n1. **II metric** (Eq 1): II_i(s,e) = Δ_compound,i − (Δ_prune,i + Δ_evict,i). This is the per-problem interaction term from a 2×2 factorial design.\n2. **Phase stratification** (Eq 2): G(s,e) = median_i(II_low,i) − median_i(II_high,i), where low=0-33% tokens, high=67-100% tokens.\n3. **Phase gradient universality**: within-importance dz > 0.95 across 3 architectures.\n4. **Strategy specificity is context-dependent**: 15–36× on MATH-500, 2.3–3.8× on GSM8K, 1.1–1.3× on GPQA; absent on LLaMA.\n5. **Discrimination collapse**: Gini–II within-model ρ=1.0 (4 points), cross-model r=0.48 (NS).\n\n## Key Related Work the Paper Must Differentiate From\n- **Physics of KV Cache Compression** (2603.01426): Theoretical framework for compression-rate phase transitions and representational rigidity. Their \"phase\" = critical compression thresholds; this paper's \"phase\" = temporal reasoning stages. But the practical insight is similar: \"compression hurts more at certain stages.\"\n- **HIES**: Identifies importance score collapse in single-axis settings with entropy compensation. This paper's \"discrimination collapse\" extends to compound setting — but is this a straightforward extension?\n- **ThinKV**: CoT-phase-aware KV cache eviction on single axis. Already does phase-aware compression.\n- **SparK / SLiM**: Joint head pruning + KV eviction but treat interaction as uniform.\n- **Ada-KV**: Adaptive cache budget allocation without phase stratification.\n- **R-KV**: KV compression optimized for reasoning tasks.\n\n## Experimental Coverage Assessment\nFrom the review context YAML, the paper covers:\n- 3 architectures (DeepSeek-Qwen, Qwen, DeepSeek-LLaMA) — all 7B scale\n- 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond)\n- Strategy specificity collapses on GPQA (ratio 1.1-1.3×) and on LLaMA (dz<0.20)\n- The narrative had to be reframed from \"strategy×phase interaction as core finding\" to \"phase gradient as universal, strategy specificity as one context-dependent manifestation\"\n- Cross-model Gini-II correlation is NS (r=0.48)\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points)\nFocus on positioning, clarity of contribution, related work coverage.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points)\nFocus on:\n- **Novelty concern 1**: Physics of KV Cache Compression already characterizes phase-dependent compression behavior. This paper's phase gradient in compound setting could be seen as a special case.\n- **Novelty concern 2**: HIES already identified importance score collapse. \"Discrimination collapse\" in compound setting is a predictable extension.\n- **Novelty concern 3**: The II metric is just the standard interaction term from factorial experimental design — calling it \"Interaction Index\" doesn't add methodological novelty.\n- **Scope concern**: Strategy specificity (initially a core claim) doesn't hold on LLaMA or GPQA, reducing the three-dimensional (compound × phase × strategy) framing to two dimensions (compound × phase), which is closer to what Physics of KV Cache already covers.\n- **Scale**: Only 7B models. No evidence this generalizes to 13B, 70B, or MoE architectures.\n- **Related work positioning**: The \"orthogonal dimension\" argument is clever but may not convince reviewers — the practical insight (\"compression hurts more in early reasoning phases\") overlaps substantially with phase-aware compression literature.\n- **Broken references**: Multiple \"??\" and \"?\" in citations throughout the paper.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n### 5. Scores\n- Soundness (1-5)\n- Excitement (1-5)\n- Overall Assessment (1-5)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the contribution clears the novelty bar for a top-tier venue with ~22% acceptance rate. Consider whether this is better suited for Findings track or a workshop."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012pREfH3yt3uFaMYKGsht9g","session_id":"93355213-87f8-4b5a-b04b-1b0ebb649ece","uuid":"766304b3-857d-494f-bea7-3c123abc1881","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:33:58.248Z"}
